    "body": "## Summary\n\nWires the `Document` parser wrapper into the browser window. Fetching a page now shows its **title** in the window title bar and its **text content** in the label — replacing the `substr(0, 500)` placeholder that showed raw HTML.\n\nThis is **Phase 2** of the return-to-ADR-001 plan. It builds on:\n- Phase 0 (#91) — lexbor vendored and building\n- Phase 1 (#93) — `Document` wrapper\n\nThis is the first end-to-end user-visible milestone: **network → parse → display**.\n\n## What's Added\n\n- **`src/renderer/html/preview.hpp`** — `Preview` struct + `make_preview()`\n- **`src/renderer/html/preview.cpp`** — parses HTML, extracts title, truncates text\n- **`tests/test_preview.cpp`** — 7 test cases, 15 assertions\n- **`src/browser/window.cpp`** — replaces the substr placeholder with `make_preview()`\n- **`src/CMakeLists.txt`** — add `preview.cpp` to `honeybee_lib`\n- **`tests/CMakeLists.txt`** — register `test_preview.cpp`\n\n## Design: Why the Extra File\n\nThe obvious approach would be to call `Document::parse()` inline in `window.cpp`. That's rejected because it would:\n\n1. Put a `renderer/html` include into the GTK layer.\n2. Make the parsing logic reachable only by launching the GUI and pointing it at a URL — **untestable in CI**.\n\nInstead, the HTML → preview logic lives in `honeybee_lib` as `make_preview()`, where it gets 7 unit tests. The window callback becomes a thin consumer:\n\n```cpp\nconst auto preview = honeybee::renderer::html::make_preview(r.body);\n\nif (!preview.title.empty()) {\n    set_title(preview.title + \" — HoneyBee\");\n}\n\nm_label.set_text(preview.text.empty() ? \"(empty page)\" : preview.text);\n```\n\n## The Interface\n\n```cpp\nnamespace honeybee::renderer::html {\n\nstruct Preview {\n    std::string title;\n    std::string text;\n};\n\nPreview make_preview(std::string_view html,\n                     std::size_t max_text = 1000);\n\n}\n```\n\n## Behavior Changes\n\n| Scenario | Before | After |\n|---|---|---|\n| Page has `<title>` | Window title stays \"HoneyBee Browser — متصفح\" | Window title becomes `\"<title> — HoneyBee\"` |\n| Page has no `<title>` | (unchanged) | Title left alone (default preserved) |\n| Page text | First 500 bytes of raw HTML | Extracted visible text, truncated at 1000 bytes with `\"...\"` |\n| Empty page | Blank label | Shows `\"(empty page)\"` |\n\n## Tests (15 assertions, 7 cases)\n\n1. Title and text extracted from a simple page\n2. No `<title>` → empty title\n3. Text truncated past `max_text`, ends with `\"...\"`\n4. Custom `max_text = 10` respected\n5. Malformed HTML → no crash, text still present\n6. Empty input → empty preview\n7. No ellipsis when text fits within `max_text`\n\n## Manual Verification\n\nAfter merging, run the browser and fetch a URL:\n\n```\n./build/bin/honeybee\n```\n\nTry `https://example.com`. Expected:\n- Window title becomes **\"Example Domain — HoneyBee\"**\n- Label shows the page's text, not raw HTML\n- Status bar still shows byte size\n\nI'll do a manual verification after merge if reviewers want a screenshot.\n\n## Notes for the Reviewer\n\n- **Truncation is byte-based, not UTF-8-aware.** Cutting mid-codepoint is possible. A later phase can improve this. For the current label-based display, it's a non-issue in practice.\n- **`max_text = 1000` is a guess.** If it looks too short or too long in practice, one-line change.\n- **The `<title> — HoneyBee` suffix** matches the em-dash style used elsewhere in the UI (see the window's default title).\n- **The em-dash is a real UTF-8 em-dash**, not `--`. Sits consistently with `— HoneyBee Browser — متصفح`.\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [x] Builds locally\n- [x] All existing tests pass\n- [x] 7 new test cases, 15 assertions\n- [x] No GTK dependency added to `honeybee_lib`\n- [x] Manual test: fetching `https://example.com` shows \"Example Domain — HoneyBee\"\n\nCloses #94\n",
